  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (RNA, pca_n2000, singleprofile) achieves 0.485 accuracy while config_010 (image, resnet50) achieves only 0.208, establishing RNA as the dominant modality. However, we have only one RNA data point, so we cannot yet assess whether the RNA encoder choice (PCA vs. learned representations) is a meaningful design lever. Question: config_004 (scvi_n200, singleprofile, rna_only) changes exactly one variable from config_001—the RNA encoder from PCA to scVI—while holding profile setting and all other factors constant. This cleanly isolates the effect of a learned, biologically-informed representation versus a linear dimensionality reduction. Why this is the best next experiment: With only 2 completed runs, the highest-value uncertainty to resolve is whether the RNA encoder matters at all. If scVI substantially outperforms PCA, it justifies investing subsequent budget in scgpt and in multiprofile/aggregation variants built on the stronger encoder. If it does not, PCA is sufficient and we can redirect budget toward profile setting, aggregation, or multimodal fusion. A single-variable comparison is the most efficient use of a limited experiment slot."
}

